WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.320
Charges about the alleged evils of dark money,&nbsp;
otherwise known as anonymous charitable giving,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.737
they're rife these days. Senator Sheldon&nbsp;
Whitehouse cites a "multi-hundred million

00:00:14.737 --> 00:00:20.320
dollar covert operation" to promote conservative&nbsp;
judicial nominations through donor-advised funds,

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.642
a fast-growing means of individual charitable&nbsp;
giving that have become a target of progressive ire.

00:00:25.642 --> 00:00:30.160
The reality is far different. In my latest&nbsp;
paper, I've reviewed grant data from the largest

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.520
donor-advised fund sponsors in the US. It turns&nbsp;
out that anonymous giving represents but a small

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:40.400
fraction of their donor's overall giving. Some&nbsp;
Americans do prefer to give anonymously due

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:45.862
to religious or cultural reasons. Most&nbsp;
don't. And public-policy-related giving — that

00:00:45.862 --> 00:00:50.480
represents an even smaller percentage,&nbsp;
both of anonymous and overall giving.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:55.280
And conservative organizations — they're not even&nbsp;
the leading recipients of anonymous DAF money.

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:02.640
The top 10 anonymously supported grantees in 2020&nbsp;
included the SPLC, Planned Parenthood, and the ACLU.

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:08.112
Memo to Senator Whitehouse: Dark money's
not what&nbsp;you think it is.

00:01:08.112 --> 00:01:12.026
Are you concerned about dark money? Let us know in our poll.

00:01:12.026 --> 00:01:16.952
Also, let us know what&nbsp;other topics
you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds

00:01:16.952 --> 00:01:22.560
and be sure to like and&nbsp;
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

